      "narration": [
        "Seventy years of AI research taught one bitter lesson."
      ],
      "data": {
        "title": "The Bitter Lesson",
        "subtitle": "General methods that leverage computation win, by a large margin",
        "kicker": "Rich Sutton · March 13, 2019"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "chess",
      "type": "compare",
      "role": "how",
      "duration_s": 8,
      "narration": [
        "Start with chess, the essay's first example.",
        "One camp built in human know-how; the other, deep search.",
        "In nineteen ninety-seven, deep search beat the world champion, Kasparov.",
        "Critics called it brute force: not how people play."
      ],
      "data": {
        "title": "Chess: two bets",
        "left": {
          "label": "Human knowledge",
          "role": "c1"
        },
        "right": {
          "label": "Deep search",
          "role": "c2"
        },
        "rows": [
          {
            "left": "Built in how people play",
            "right": "Massive, deep search",
            "winner": "none"
          },
          {
            "left": "Not what won",
            "right": "Beat Kasparov, 1997",
            "winner": "right"
          }
        ],
        "footer": "Critics: \"brute force, not how people play\""
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "why_search",
      "type": "metrics",
      "role": "why",
      "duration_s": 8,
      "narration": [
        "So why did search win?",
        "Most research assumed the computing power it had was fixed.",
        "But the cost of computation keeps falling, exponentially.",
        "Soon, far more of it becomes available.",
        "Time spent on knowledge is time not spent on computation."
      ],
      "data": {
        "title": "Why search won",
        "cards": [
          {
            "label": "Research assumed",
            "value": "Fixed",
            "compare": "computing power"
          },
          {
            "label": "In fact",
            "value": "Falling",
            "compare": "cost per unit: Moore's law"
          },
          {
            "label": "Soon after",
            "value": "Far more",
            "compare": "computation available"
          }
        ],
        "footnote": "Time on one is time not spent on the other"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pattern",
      "type": "cycle",
      "role": "how",
      "duration_s": 20,
      "narration": [
        "Chess wasn't a one-off; the same four steps keep coming round.",
        "Researchers build what they know into their programs.",
        "That helps at first, and it's satisfying.",
        "Then it levels off, and even gets in the way.",
        "The breakthrough comes from search and learning, scaled up.",
        "Chess went round in nineteen ninety-seven.",
        "Go went round twenty years later, once search ran at scale.",
        "In speech, statistical methods beat hand-built knowledge of words and sounds.",
        "In vision, deep networks replaced hand-designed features."
      ],
